WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.583 --> 00:00:03.503
There are about 1.5 billion cows 
on this planet.

00:00:03.878 --> 00:00:06.339
That's one cow for every 
five people on Earth.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.842
We keep them for dairy... 
and meat...

00:00:09.342 --> 00:00:12.429
and they’re a climate problem
that we've struggled to solve.

00:00:12.762 --> 00:00:15.098
Every year, each one of those cows 
is estimated

00:00:15.098 --> 00:00:18.518
to release up to 220 pounds
of methane...

00:00:18.977 --> 00:00:20.562
mostly when they burp.

00:00:20.937 --> 00:00:25.233
That's about 4% of all greenhouse
gas emissions that are warming our planet...

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:27.485
from burping cows.

00:00:28.236 --> 00:00:30.155
You've probably heard 
a few solutions to this

00:00:30.155 --> 00:00:32.866
like trying to convince people 
to lower their beef intake...

00:00:33.158 --> 00:00:34.909
or switch to meat alternatives.

00:00:35.744 --> 00:00:39.456
But instead of trying to convince 
billions of humans to change their diets

00:00:39.456 --> 00:00:42.876
some researchers are focusing on 
the diet of the cows.

00:00:43.376 --> 00:00:46.755
And a promising solution might 
come in the form...

00:00:48.089 --> 00:00:49.132
of seaweed.

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:56.473
This video is presented 
by Delta Airlines.

00:00:59.142 --> 00:01:01.644
One of the reasons cows produce
so much methane

00:01:01.644 --> 00:01:03.772
is because of their specialized 
digestive tract.

00:01:04.314 --> 00:01:06.649
It allows them to digest 
tough plant material

00:01:06.649 --> 00:01:09.027
like grass, hay, and other vegetation.

00:01:09.569 --> 00:01:11.780
Their stomach consists of four compartments.

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:13.156
The largest one, 
the rumen

00:01:13.156 --> 00:01:16.910
acts sort of as a storage unit where 
food can sit and ferment.

00:01:16.993 --> 00:01:19.204
Microbes in this area break down the food

00:01:19.204 --> 00:01:21.873
using a process called enteric fermentation.

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:25.668
This process provides the animal 
with the nutrients and energy it needs

00:01:25.668 --> 00:01:28.546
but it also produces methane as waste...

00:01:28.838 --> 00:01:30.548
and the cow burps it out...

00:01:30.799 --> 00:01:33.760
which makes the Earth 
a little bit hotter.

00:01:34.719 --> 00:01:37.430
Here's the thing, though 
methane is literally waste...

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:39.641
and changing the diet of the cow 
can change

00:01:39.641 --> 00:01:41.142
how much of it is produced.

00:01:41.643 --> 00:01:44.145
Foods like grass or hay 
stay in the rumen longer

00:01:44.145 --> 00:01:45.396
and produce more of it.

00:01:45.647 --> 00:01:49.567
Whereas carbohydrate rich foods
like corn produce less methane.

00:01:50.276 --> 00:01:52.487
But the goal here isn't to completely change

00:01:52.487 --> 00:01:55.115
the diet of all of these cows 
that we're feeding.

00:01:55.448 --> 00:01:57.283
We can just tweak it a little.

00:01:58.952 --> 00:02:01.704
We've been doing a lot of work 
on feed additives

00:02:01.704 --> 00:02:03.665
to try to reduce synthetic 
methane emissions.

00:02:03.915 --> 00:02:06.793
Ermias Kebreab is the associate dean 
at UC Davis

00:02:06.793 --> 00:02:09.629
in the College of Agriculture 
and Environmental Sciences.

00:02:10.046 --> 00:02:12.340
In 2018, he led a study that tested

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:14.717
how cows reacted to a certain additive...

00:02:15.051 --> 00:02:15.677
Seaweed.

00:02:16.052 --> 00:02:17.887
Specifically red seaweed.

00:02:18.012 --> 00:02:21.641
Which grows naturally in tropical 
and subtropical ocean regions.

00:02:22.183 --> 00:02:25.645
Back in 2016, researchers in Australia
had found that sprinkling

00:02:25.645 --> 00:02:28.064
the seaweed on cow feed 
could potentially reduce

00:02:28.064 --> 00:02:30.859
methane emissions by over 95%.

00:02:31.317 --> 00:02:34.070
And in 2021, Kebreab and his team were able

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:36.823
to replicate the study in live animals in California.

00:02:37.198 --> 00:02:41.119
That completely blew my mind 
when I saw how effective it was.

00:02:41.286 --> 00:02:43.538
The final results found that 
the seaweed could reduce

00:02:43.538 --> 00:02:45.999
methane emissions by 82%.

00:02:46.457 --> 00:02:48.459
At first I couldn't believe it because I...

00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:51.129
I've never seen anything like that before.

00:02:52.255 --> 00:02:54.424
And this specific species of red seaweed contains

00:02:54.424 --> 00:02:56.718
high amounts of a compound 
called bromoform.

00:02:57.302 --> 00:02:58.344
When a cow ingests it

00:02:58.344 --> 00:03:00.430
it suppresses the enzyme 
that makes methane.

00:03:01.097 --> 00:03:01.973
And the cows

00:03:01.973 --> 00:03:04.434
they don't even notice the change 
in their dinners.

00:03:04.726 --> 00:03:05.727
The seaweed is dried

00:03:05.727 --> 00:03:07.854
and sprinkled onto the food 
they're used to.

00:03:08.104 --> 00:03:10.064
They can still get all the nutrients they need.

00:03:10.064 --> 00:03:11.941
Produce milk and grow.

00:03:11.941 --> 00:03:13.359
But the waste is reduced.

00:03:13.818 --> 00:03:16.196
Other additives work too, 
like fatty acids

00:03:16.196 --> 00:03:18.865
which reduce methane by lowering 
the carbohydrate content

00:03:18.865 --> 00:03:22.243
and reducing a specific parasite in the rumen 
known as protozoa.

00:03:22.452 --> 00:03:24.162
And oregano was found to reduce

00:03:24.162 --> 00:03:25.580
the number of bacteria in the rumen

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:27.123
to limit methane production.

00:03:27.665 --> 00:03:30.001
But so far, out of all the additives tested

00:03:30.001 --> 00:03:32.003
seaweed has been the most effective.

00:03:32.587 --> 00:03:35.173
The best news is that scaling up 
the growth of seaweed

00:03:35.173 --> 00:03:37.300
doesn’t technically drain 
other resources

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:39.093
like fresh water or fertilizer...

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:41.346
since we can grow it in the ocean.

00:03:41.888 --> 00:03:44.015
Growing seaweed could also 
combat other issues

00:03:44.015 --> 00:03:45.308
like ocean acidification...

00:03:45.475 --> 00:03:48.770
another climate problem caused by 
rising levels of carbon dioxide.

00:03:49.395 --> 00:03:51.981
But there's still a lot of research left to do...

00:03:52.523 --> 00:03:55.109
including figuring out how to 
dry it and package it

00:03:55.109 --> 00:03:56.694
and ship it at a low cost.

00:03:56.986 --> 00:03:58.738
Like, how do we effectively 
get seaweed

00:03:58.738 --> 00:04:01.616
from a subtropical area 
to, say... Maine...

00:04:01.908 --> 00:04:04.202
without adding tons of emissions 
with shipping?

00:04:04.202 --> 00:04:08.539
That is, if you didn't know that seaweed 
is super heavy when wet.

00:04:09.415 --> 00:04:11.834
But once we figure out the logistics, 
it's over.

00:04:11.834 --> 00:04:12.627
We solved it.

00:04:12.627 --> 00:04:15.255
The cows are content and 
the planet is saved.

00:04:16.005 --> 00:04:18.758
Except not at all, obviously.

00:04:19.467 --> 00:04:22.762
For one, this doesn't appear to be 
a one size fits all solution.

00:04:23.012 --> 00:04:24.681
Recently, another Australian study

00:04:24.681 --> 00:04:28.768
found that the same red seaweed only cut 
methane emissions by under 30%.

00:04:29.227 --> 00:04:31.980
Now, granted, that's still a reduction, 
which is good news

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:34.357
but may be less of a miracle 
than we hoped.

00:04:34.941 --> 00:04:37.986
Others are urging researchers 
to slow down on red seaweed

00:04:37.986 --> 00:04:39.320
because of the bromoform .

00:04:39.529 --> 00:04:41.572
In it’s pure form, 
the EPA has reported

00:04:41.572 --> 00:04:43.116
that it could be dangerous 
to human health

00:04:43.116 --> 00:04:44.325
if ingested.

00:04:44.534 --> 00:04:47.704
Luckily, studies so far haven't found 
that at dangerous levels in milk

00:04:47.704 --> 00:04:50.206
or in meat from cows eating 
the seaweed additive.

00:04:50.623 --> 00:04:52.417
But studies still need to be done 

00:04:52.417 --> 00:04:55.003
on how it might affect 
the cows long term.

00:04:55.461 --> 00:04:57.630
So it's not a silver bullet...

00:04:58.131 --> 00:05:00.717
but thankfully seaweed’s 
not our only option.

00:05:00.883 --> 00:05:03.720
I think this is just one of several solutions

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:05.054
that are being looked at 
at the moment.

00:05:05.513 --> 00:05:08.224
And overall, there's a big push for change.

00:05:08.558 --> 00:05:12.270
In 2021, 150 countries, 
including the United States

00:05:12.270 --> 00:05:14.564
signed the United Nations 
Global Methane Pledge...

00:05:14.772 --> 00:05:18.401
which aims to reduce methane emissions 
by 30% by 2030.

00:05:18.776 --> 00:05:21.821
It's becoming more and more clear now 
that we won't get there...

00:05:22.238 --> 00:05:25.033
unless we tackle enteric 
methane emissions.

00:05:25.408 --> 00:05:28.661
In an ideal world, 
we'd have less livestock.

00:05:28.995 --> 00:05:30.705
But getting people to change their habits

00:05:30.705 --> 00:05:33.791
isn't always the most straightforward 
or quickest solution.

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:36.627
The work of searching for solutions
to the climate crisis

00:05:36.627 --> 00:05:38.046
needs to be multifaceted.

00:05:38.463 --> 00:05:40.465
Within a year or two, 
you will see quite

00:05:40.465 --> 00:05:42.884
a lot of different activities 
and different solutions.

00:05:44.052 --> 00:05:45.219
That's going to come up.

00:05:45.553 --> 00:05:48.097
And that could be one less 
cow burp at a time.

